WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.060
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online IELTS preparation.

2
00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:08.886
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.186 --> 00:00:17.248
Unit four: Listening: Notes and Summary Completion. Today you will follow a lecture and complete notes in Sections 3 and 4.

4
00:00:17.548 --> 00:00:20.166
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:24.449
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.283 --> 00:00:26.296
Lecture.

7
00:00:27.283 --> 00:00:29.724
Lecture means a formal talk to students.

8
00:00:30.317 --> 00:00:32.049
The lecture is about climate.

9
00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:35.360
Your turn. Say: Lecture.

10
00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:39.120
Lecture.

11
00:00:39.883 --> 00:00:41.035
Signpost.

12
00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:45.141
Signpost means a phrase that shows the structure of a talk.

13
00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:47.413
Firstly is a signpost.

14
00:00:48.017 --> 00:00:50.553
Your turn. Say: Signpost.

15
00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:54.552
Signpost.

16
00:00:55.317 --> 00:00:56.329
Summary.

17
00:00:57.317 --> 00:01:00.082
Summary means a short version of the main points.

18
00:01:00.683 --> 00:01:02.056
Complete the summary.

19
00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:05.244
Your turn. Say: Summary.

20
00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:09.159
Summary.

21
00:01:09.917 --> 00:01:10.686
Keyword.

22
00:01:11.683 --> 00:01:14.565
Keyword means an important word that carries meaning.

23
00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:16.662
Underline each keyword.

24
00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.299
Your turn. Say: Keyword.

25
00:01:22.133 --> 00:01:23.053
Keyword.

26
00:01:23.817 --> 00:01:24.829
Sequence.

27
00:01:25.817 --> 00:01:27.781
Sequence means the order of events.

28
00:01:28.383 --> 00:01:30.127
Note the sequence of steps.

29
00:01:30.717 --> 00:01:33.241
Your turn. Say: Sequence.

30
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:37.194
Sequence.

31
00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:38.823
Research.

32
00:01:39.817 --> 00:01:42.803
Research means careful study to find facts.

33
00:01:43.417 --> 00:01:45.312
The research took two years.

34
00:01:45.917 --> 00:01:49.208
Your turn. Say: Research.

35
00:01:52.067 --> 00:01:53.137
Research.

36
00:01:53.883 --> 00:01:54.792
Seminar.

37
00:01:55.783 --> 00:01:58.421
Seminar means a small class for discussion.

38
00:01:59.017 --> 00:02:00.830
They prepare for a seminar.

39
00:02:01.417 --> 00:02:03.848
Your turn. Say: Seminar.

40
00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:07.782
Seminar.

41
00:02:08.517 --> 00:02:09.274
Tutor.

42
00:02:10.283 --> 00:02:13.374
Tutor means a teacher who guides one or a few students.

43
00:02:13.983 --> 00:02:15.518
The tutor gives feedback.

44
00:02:16.117 --> 00:02:18.421
Your turn. Say: Tutor.

45
00:02:21.267 --> 00:02:22.024
Tutor.

46
00:02:22.833 --> 00:02:26.078
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:26.917 --> 00:02:30.727
Visal, how are Sections 3 and 4 different from Section 1?

48
00:02:31.817 --> 00:02:42.283
Section 3 is a discussion between students and a tutor. Section 4 is one long academic lecture with no break, so you must follow the structure.

49
00:02:43.383 --> 00:02:45.313
What helps you follow a lecture?

50
00:02:46.417 --> 00:02:56.348
Listen for signposts such as firstly, however and in conclusion. They tell you when the speaker adds a point, changes direction or finishes.

51
00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:59.078
How do I complete notes?

52
00:03:00.183 --> 00:03:10.034
Read the headings first, then predict the missing word type. Write only the words you hear, and check the spelling and the word limit.

53
00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:13.107
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:18.330
Grammar focus: Strategy: follow the structure of the talk.

55
00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:19.724
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:20.417 --> 00:03:23.844
Signposts like however show that the speaker changes direction.

57
00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:28.201
Read the headings first, then predict each missing word.

58
00:03:29.167 --> 00:03:36.927
In a lecture, the notes follow the order of the audio. Use the headings as a map, listen for signposts, and answer in the order you hear them.

59
00:03:37.683 --> 00:03:41.311
Be careful! Don't say: I ignore however in a lecture.

60
00:03:43.267 --> 00:03:46.508
Say: I listen for however as a signpost.

61
00:03:46.808 --> 00:03:48.750
Signposts show structure.

62
00:03:49.533 --> 00:03:52.255
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

63
00:03:53.067 --> 00:03:54.985
A Marine Biology Lecture.

64
00:03:55.285 --> 00:03:57.644
The lecturer talks about coral reefs.

65
00:03:58.633 --> 00:04:01.028
Firstly, she explains how corals grow.

66
00:04:02.033 --> 00:04:05.426
However, recent research shows that warm water damages them.

67
00:04:06.433 --> 00:04:10.511
In conclusion, protecting reefs needs local action and global agreement.

68
00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:14.591
Students complete a summary with no more than two words.

69
00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:19.655
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

70
00:04:20.517 --> 00:04:22.330
Signposts help you blank.

71
00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:27.155
A, find maps. B, follow the structure. C, spell names.

72
00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:33.094
The answer is B: follow the structure.

73
00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:36.296
The word however usually shows blank.

74
00:04:36.596 --> 00:04:41.155
A, the end of the test. B, a spelling. C, a change of direction.

75
00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:47.385
The answer is C: a change of direction.

76
00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:50.642
The notes follow the order of the blank.

77
00:04:50.942 --> 00:04:54.468
A, audio. B, options. C, answers.

78
00:04:58.033 --> 00:05:00.184
The answer is A: audio.

79
00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:04.987
What damages corals according to recent research?

80
00:05:05.287 --> 00:05:09.010
A, new roads. B, warm water. C, cold air.

81
00:05:12.567 --> 00:05:14.682
The answer is B: warm water.

82
00:05:15.817 --> 00:05:17.224
What is Section 4?

83
00:05:17.524 --> 00:05:21.979
A, a phone call. B, a map task. C, a long lecture.

84
00:05:25.533 --> 00:05:27.986
The answer is C: a long lecture.

85
00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:32.386
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

86
00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:36.248
How do you follow a long lecture in the Listening test?

87
00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:46.515
Now listen to Visal.

88
00:05:46.815 --> 00:06:04.714
I read the headings first so I know the structure. Then I listen for signposts like firstly, however and in conclusion. I write only the words I hear, I check the word limit, and I keep listening even if I miss one answer.

89
00:06:05.014 --> 00:06:06.805
Excellent answer, Visal!

90
00:06:07.717 --> 00:06:13.097
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and strategy: follow the structure of the talk.

91
00:06:13.397 --> 00:06:17.698
Today's value is perseverance. Keep trying, even when it is hard.

92
00:06:17.998 --> 00:06:20.488
See you in Unit five. Goodbye!

93
00:06:20.788 --> 00:06:23.452
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

